//! `std::cout << some_float` reproduced byte for byte.
//!
//! libstdc++ implements `num_put<char>::do_put(float)` by handing the value to
//! `printf` with a `%g`-family conversion, so a default-configured `std::ostream`
//! prints a `float` exactly like `printf("%g", (double)f)`: six significant
//! digits, trailing zeros stripped, and the `%e`/`%f` style chosen from the
//! decimal exponent.
//!
//! The subtle part is rounding. `printf` rounds the *exact* binary value, with
//! ties going to even. `f32::to_string` instead prints the shortest string that
//! round-trips, so `57.0/128.0` comes out as `0.4453125` in Rust and `0.445312`
